 has shrunk to historic lows, the coalitions to prevent the AfD from taking=
 power have become increasingly tenuous.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal =
17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Saxony-Anhalt is =
a case in point. The only way to keep the AfD out of power is to have all o=
ther five wildly disparate parties join forces, from the conservative cente=
r-right all the way to the post-Communist far left (they=E2=80=99re the wav=
ering ones.). Their only common ground: Keep out the AfD.</span></p><p styl=
e=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=
That=E2=80=99s not the only way mainstream parties have sought to avoid giv=
ing the party power.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,se=
rif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>When the AfD first entered parliament=
 in 2017, the rules were hastily changed to give the post of honorary head =
of the legislature to the longest-serving lawmaker rather than the eldest, =
who was an AfD lawmaker. Taxpayer funds traditionally allocated to a party =
for an affiliated foundation were frozen when the AfD created one. The chai=
rs of parliamentary committees were traditionally appointed based on vote s=
hare, with the approval of other parties treated as a formality; not anymor=
e.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;mar=
gin:0 0 15px"><span>When it became clear this spring that the AfD might win=
 a majority in Saxony-Anhalt, one of its East German strongholds, the state=
=E2=80=99s governing coalition passed a law designed to stop any one party =
from vetoing judicial appointments or blocking the appointment of a governo=
r it does not like.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,ser=
if;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>And as my </span><a href=3D"https://nl=
